    id: 'ddos-attacks',
    title: 'DDoS Attacks: Amplified and Reflected',
    tagline: 'Thousands of sources flood a target; reflection hides the attacker behind innocent servers and amplification turns tiny requests into huge responses.',
    covers: ['network-attacks/ddos'],
    glossary: ['ddos', 'dos', 'botnet'],
    beginner: {
      what: 'A **denial-of-service (DoS)** attack makes a service unavailable. A **distributed** DoS (**DDoS**) uses many machines — often a **botnet** of infected computers and IoT devices — at once. In a **reflected** attack, the attacker sends requests to third-party servers with the victim’s address forged as the sender, so the replies hit the victim. In an **amplified** attack, each small request triggers a much larger response.',
      why: 'Availability is one leg of the CIA triad. A DDoS can take an online store, bank or government site offline for hours, costing money and reputation — and is sometimes a smokescreen for other attacks.',
      how: 'The attacker spoofs the victim’s IP address and sends small queries (e.g. 60-byte DNS requests asking for large records) to thousands of open servers. Each server reflects a reply of thousands of bytes to the victim. Combined, the victim receives far more traffic than its link can handle. Defences: upstream DDoS scrubbing services/CDNs, rate limiting, anycast, filtering spoofed traffic at ISPs, and fixing open resolvers.',
      where: 'Public websites, online gaming, DNS providers, banks, government services and hacktivist campaigns.',
      analogy: 'Reflection: you write a thousand letters to different shops, putting your victim’s address as the return address — all the replies flood their mailbox. Amplification: each letter says “send me your full catalogue”, so every small letter produces a huge package.',
    },
    exam: {
      what: '**DDoS**: denial of service from many distributed sources (botnets). **Reflected**: spoofed source IP makes third parties send responses to the victim, hiding the attacker. **Amplified**: protocol responses far larger than requests (DNS, NTP, memcached, SSDP, CLDAP), multiplying attack bandwidth. Types also include volumetric, protocol (SYN flood) and application-layer (HTTP floods).',
      how: 'Indicators: sudden traffic spikes, many sources, large inbound UDP responses with no matching outbound queries, resource exhaustion, service unavailability. Mitigations: DDoS protection/scrubbing services, CDNs, anycast, rate limiting, ingress/egress filtering against spoofing (BCP 38), disabling open resolvers/unneeded UDP services, auto-scaling and capacity planning.',
      scenario: 'A company’s link is saturated by 40 Gbps of DNS responses from thousands of resolvers, although its servers never sent those queries — a reflected, amplified DDoS.',
    },
    terms: [
      { term: 'Botnet', def: 'A network of compromised devices controlled by an attacker.' },
      { term: 'Spoofing', def: 'Forging the source address of packets.' },
      { term: 'Amplification factor', def: 'Ratio of response size to request size (can be 50× or more).' },
      { term: 'Scrubbing center', def: 'Provider infrastructure that filters attack traffic before it reaches the victim.' },
    ],
    example: {
      title: 'Black Friday outage',
      body: 'Hours before a sale, a retailer’s website slows to a crawl. NetFlow shows huge volumes of UDP traffic **from port 53 and 123** of thousands of servers worldwide — DNS and NTP responses to queries the retailer never made. It’s a **reflected, amplified DDoS**. The retailer activates its upstream DDoS protection, which absorbs the traffic at scrubbing centers, and the site recovers. Post-incident, it adds a CDN and documents a DDoS playbook.',
    },
    differs: [
      { vs: 'DoS vs DDoS', diff: 'DoS comes from one source; DDoS from many — much harder to block by IP.' },
      { vs: 'Reflected vs amplified', diff: 'Reflection is about *bouncing* traffic off third parties via spoofing; amplification is about *multiplying* its size. Many attacks are both.' },
    ],
    cues: ['“Responses to requests the victim never sent” → reflection.', '“Small request, huge response” (DNS, NTP, memcached) → amplification.', '“Thousands of sources” → distributed (botnet).'],
    traps: ['Blocking the source IPs of a reflected attack blocks innocent third-party servers, not the attacker.', 'A firewall at your edge can’t help once your Internet link is already saturated — mitigation must happen upstream.'],
    memory: '**Reflect = bounce (spoofed return address). Amplify = multiply (bigger reply).**',
    alt: [{ label: 'Pizza prank', text: 'Ordering 500 pizzas to someone else’s house (reflection) — and ordering the biggest family size every time (amplification).' }],
